Recognizing that the current situation in Abyei and along the border between Sudan and South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Recalling resolution 1990 (2011), resolution 1325 (2000), resolution 2024 (2011), resolution 2075 (2012), resolution 2760 (2024), resolution 2518 (2020), resolution 2589 (2021), resolution 2046 (2012), resolution 2730 (2024), resolution 1894 (2009), resolution 2467 (2019), resolution 2250 (2015), resolution 2419 (2018), resolution 2535 (2020), resolution 2378 (2017), resolution 2436 (2018), resolution 2272 (2016), and resolution 2538 (2020),

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend until 15 November 2026 the mandate of UNISFA as set out in paragraph 2 of resolution 1990 (2011), and further decides to extend until 15 November 2026 the tasks of UNISFA as set out in paragraph 3 of resolution 1990 (2011);

2. Encourages demonstrable progress by Sudan and South Sudan on the following benchmarks, which should be reasonable and realistic, should be developed in close consultation with Sudan, South Sudan, troop- and police-contributing countries and relevant stakeholders, and should focus on implementation of the UNISFA mandate and full cooperation with the mission, and notes that such progress should include, inter alia, efforts to:
(a) Within their respective purviews, urge all unauthorized armed forces to withdraw from Abyei and fully demilitarize the area;
(b) Resume and document meetings of the Joint Political and Security Mechanism (JPSM) and the Abyei Joint Oversight Committee (AJOC);
(c) Establish the Abyei Police Service;
(d) Issue visas for UNISFA police personnel without delay;
(e) Support the appointment of a civilian deputy head of mission;

3. Reaffirms that the benchmarks set out in paragraph 2 above are indicative and shall not constitute an automatic precondition for the Mission’s renewal, drawdown or adjustment, and shall not be used as automatic preconditions; that any decision regarding the Mission’s future remains subject to the Council’s determination, taking into account conditions on the ground, including the implications for the protection of civilians, and the views of the Governments of Sudan and South Sudan, troop- and police-contributing countries, and the African Union and IGAD; and that any future adjustment to UNISFA’s mandate, configuration or drawdown shall be conditions-based and shall be undertaken in close consultation with the Governments of Sudan and South Sudan, troop- and police-contributing countries, and the African Union and IGAD;

4. Demands that Sudan and South Sudan fully comply with the 2011 Agreement and relevant Security Council resolutions, including by withdrawing all unauthorized forces from Abyei and ensuring that the Abyei Area remains a demilitarized and weapons-free zone;

5. Urges the Governments of Sudan and South Sudan to resume and document meetings of the JPSM and the AJOC without further delay, and to engage in substantive dialogue, in coordination with UNISFA, to advance the political process for resolution of the Abyei dispute, including the establishment of the Abyei Police Service and other joint institutions;

6. Calls upon Sudan to issue visas without delay for UNISFA police personnel, including the three formed police units and corrections officers, and upon all parties to remove obstacles that prevent UNISFA from fully executing its mandate and to comply with the Status of Forces Agreements, and calls upon South Sudan to facilitate without delay the redeployment of UNISFA personnel to Gok Machar;

7. Demands the immediate return of the UNISFA trucks and fuel seized in February 2025 and the immediate cessation of all attacks against United Nations personnel, including direct firing at UN helicopters, and reiterates that such attacks may constitute war crimes and that those responsible should be swiftly and thoroughly investigated and held to account;

8. Requests UNISFA, within its mandate and in coordination with relevant United Nations agencies, to continue to support community dialogue and reconciliation, including between the Misseriya and Ngok Dinka, Ngok Dinka and Twic Dinka, and all other communities, and to support the safe coordination of displaced persons and humanitarian access, while ensuring that all activities are undertaken in a conflict-sensitive manner;

9. Demands that all parties allow full, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to civilians in Abyei and the border areas, and calls upon all parties to protect humanitarian personnel, facilities and assets and to facilitate the delivery of essential services;

10. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, to protect civilians, including children, and to prevent and respond to conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, and stresses the need for accountability for all violations and abuses;

11. Requests UNISFA to promote the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in peace processes and to consider climate-related security risks in its analysis and reporting, consistent with its mandate;

12. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution, including progress by the Governments of Sudan and South Sudan on the benchmarks set out in paragraph 2 above, and on the protection of civilians and the humanitarian situation, and to include in those reports assessments of the mission’s impact and any recommendations for mandate adjustments;

13. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

